new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2f88ac6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/BUILD-INSTALL-ON-IPAD.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Install on iPad
+
+Private, non-commercial source build based on SideInstaller v2.0.1 (`50b2a0dea22d43659e2630e01daa9097145874cb`). SideInstaller by FrizzleM. See [upstream](https://github.com/FrizzleM/SideInstaller) and [the original license](LICENSE.md).
+
+## Build the unsigned IPA
+
+The source ZIP includes `.github/workflows/build-personal-ipad.yml`. Use a private GitHub repository with Actions and the `xcode-27` runner available. Extract the archive, publish the contents as the repository root on `main`, and open **Actions → Build Install on iPad**. The first push starts the build. Use **Run workflow** to retry. Download the `Install-on-iPad` artifact from the workflow run and extract `Install-on-iPad.ipa`. The simulator test step checks saved pairing records, Bonjour matching and the refresh archive.
+
+On an Apple Silicon Mac with Xcode 27.2 or newer, the iOS 27.2 or newer device SDK, XcodeGen and Rust, you can build the same unsigned IPA from a clean copy of this directory. The workflow selects a compatible Xcode explicitly and prints the Xcode build and SDK versions in the log.
+
+```bash
+PERSONAL_IPAD_INSTALLER=1 \
+BUNDLE_ID=com.personaltools.installonipad \
+VERSION=1.1.0 \
+OUTPUT=build/Install-on-iPad.ipa \
+bash scripts/build-ipa.sh
+```
+
+## Pair and install
+
+1. Sign and install `Install-on-iPad.ipa` with Signulous on the iPhone. Open the app on the iPhone and grant Local Network access when asked.
+2. Put both devices on the same Wi-Fi network with IPv4 enabled. Turn on Developer Mode on the iPad and keep it unlocked during the first install.
+3. Add the Apple Account that should sign apps for the iPad, choose an IPA, and tap **Install on iPad**. Approve the pairing code on the iPad under **Settings → Privacy & Security → Developer Mode**. Trust the signing account on the iPad if prompted.
+4. On later installs, choose an IPA and tap **Install on iPad** again. The app reconnects with the saved pairing record and uses Bonjour to look up the iPad when its previous address or port stops working. Unlock the iPad before retrying a failed connection. Use **Forget iPad** if the iPad has explicitly discarded the pairing or you want to switch devices.
+
+The iPhone’s Signulous registration is used for the installer. The Apple Account signs the IPAs for the iPad. With a free Apple Account, each profile expires after seven days and the three-app limit still applies.
+
+## Refresh and Increased Memory Limit
+
+Tap **Refresh** next to an app in **Refresh iPad apps**. The installer keeps a private copy of the original IPA on the iPhone. It uses the same Apple Account and checks the signed app identity before reinstalling over the existing app. The list shows the expiry from the issued profile. For an app installed before this build, choose its original IPA and install it again with the same signing account to add a refresh copy. Use the app’s options menu to remove the saved copy from the iPhone.
+
+Turn on **Request Increased Memory Limit** when choosing an IPA, or select **Refresh with Increased Memory Limit** from a saved app’s options. If Apple refuses the capability or the signed profile lacks it, the attempt stops before sending the app. The result reports when the profile grants it. Future refreshes request it again for that app. The toggle requests the capability when enabled. It leaves any capability already on the App ID in place when disabled. Actual memory availability depends on iPadOS and the device.
+
+## Changes and hardware checks
+
+This build opens straight to the IPA picker and saves one iPad pairing in the app’s private storage. It validates the saved iPad’s UDID before signing and again before transfer. An unsuccessful connection leaves the pairing in place. A successful transfer closes the tunnel and removes the staged copy of the selected IPA. The first pairing is saved before signing, so a sign-in failure can be retried without another PIN.
+
+After installing the dedicated build, test an initial transfer with a small IPA, force-quit and reopen the installer, then refresh the app. Confirm the app's data remains and check the updated profile expiry. Repeat with Increased Memory Limit and after the iPad gets a new IPv4 address. Device testing is required to confirm that iPadOS accepts the pairing after a reboot and that the Wi-Fi network forwards Bonjour announcements.
